<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Muti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">914–974</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Muti li-llah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Muti was the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 946 to 974, placed on the throne by the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> after the blinding and deposition of al-Mustakfi. Throughout his reign real power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> lay with the Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and the caliph was reduced to a figurehead who conferred titles and legitimacy. Paralysed by a stroke, he abdicated in 974 in favour of his son al-Ta'i.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-muti.jpg" alt="al-Muti" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MansurISamanidCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
